 equis:{name:'Sala Equis',area:'La Latina',lat:40.4119,lng:-3.7064,address:'Calle del Duque de Alba 4',wiki:W('La Latina'),wikiLabel:'Wikipedia · neighbourhood',story:'A cinema space that also works beautifully for a beer and a conversation. We are going for the atmosphere; a film screening is a separate plan with its own ticket.',secret:'The building’s cinema past is part of the appeal. Look up from your beer and notice how the room still frames a screen.',practical:'Friday published hours 17:30–02:00. Entry depends on capacity. From Argüelles, L3 to Sol, then walk about 10 minutes.',source:SOURCES.equis,kind:'night'},
 sol:{name:'Puerta del Sol',area:'Sol',lat:40.4169,lng:-3.7035,address:'Puerta del Sol',wiki:W('Puerta del Sol'),story:'Our meeting point and compass. The older town and palace lie to the west; the Prado and Retiro lie east. In 1561 Philip II moved his court to Madrid, transforming the town’s future.',secret:'Madrid’s story did not start with the kings. Today’s walk goes backwards from the capital to the medieval settlement.',practical:'Fer: L1 direct from Pacífico. José: L5 to Gran Vía, then walk. If the square is crowded, meet by the start of Calle Mayor.',kind:'history'},
 mayor:{name:'Plaza Mayor',area:'Austrias',lat:40.4154,lng:-3.7074,address:'Plaza Mayor',wiki:W('Plaza Mayor, Madrid'),story:'A public stage built under Philip III in 1617–1619. Trade, ceremony and spectacle all fitted inside this enclosure. Much of its current appearance follows rebuilding after the fire of 1790.',secret:'Imagine every balcony occupied. The square is not just architecture: it is a machine for turning a crowd into an audience.',practical:'Free exterior stop, 15–20 min. Follow the arcades, then continue west towards the Villa.',kind:'history'},
 villa:{name:'Plaza de la Villa',area:'Austrias',lat:40.4153,lng:-3.7108,address:'Plaza de la Villa',wiki:W('Plaza de la Villa'),story:'Three centuries stand side by side: the Lujanes buildings from the 15th century, Casa de Cisneros from the 16th and the former town hall from the 17th. Madrid grew in layers.',secret:'Find the narrow Calle del Codo. That change in scale is a good way to feel the medieval town without needing a long list of dates.',practical:'Free exterior stop. Sunday’s flock reception is planned here, so this place will feel very different tomorrow.',kind:'history'},
 palace:{name:'Royal Palace & Oriente',area:'Austrias',lat:40.418,lng:-3.7143,address:'Plaza de Oriente',wiki:W('Royal Palace of Madrid'),story:'The palace on view is Bourbon and 18th-century. The Habsburg kings lived in an earlier Alcázar on the same site, destroyed by fire in 1734. Charles III moved into the new palace in 1764.',secret:'You are on a walk through Habsburg Madrid, looking at a palace built by another dynasty. The royal location survived; the building did not.',practical:'Exterior visit in the main plan. An interior visit needs a separate ticket and replaces part of the morning. Do not squeeze it in as an extra.',source:'https://www.patrimonionacional.es/visita/palacio-real-de-madrid',kind:'history'},
 wall:{name:'Madrid’s Muslim wall',area:'Cuesta de la Vega',lat:40.4158,lng:-3.7149,address:'Parque del Emir Mohamed I',wiki:W('Muslim Walls of Madrid'),story:'These surviving walls take us back to the 9th-century fortified settlement associated with Emir Muhammad I. Madrid’s urban origins are Islamic, centuries before the royal court arrived.',secret:'Look at the thickness and surviving towers. This was a defensive place before it was a capital.',practical:'A short stop below the Almudena. Then head towards Bailén and Las Vistillas.',source:'https://www.esmadrid.com/informacion-turistica/muralla-arabe',kind:'history'},
 vistillas:{name:'Las Vistillas',area:'La Latina',lat:40.4119,lng:-3.7142,address:'Jardines de Las Vistillas',wiki:'https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jardines_de_las_Vistillas',wikiLabel:'Wikipedia · Spanish',story:'The view explains the city’s geography: the old town sits high above the Manzanares valley. These gardens also belong to the history of neighbourhood festivals, including La Paloma.',secret:'The view feels timeless, but the garden design is largely 20th-century. Old Madrid includes many relatively new public spaces.',practical:'Free. A good place for a bench break before going downhill towards the river.',kind:'walk'},
 bridge:{name:'Puente de Segovia',area:'Manzanares',lat:40.4135,lng:-3.7222,address:'Puente de Segovia',wiki:'https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puente_de_Segovia',wikiLabel:'Wikipedia · Spanish',story:'A Renaissance bridge developed under Philip II, with Juan de Herrera directing work before its completion in 1584. A growing capital needed reliable routes for people and supplies.',secret:'The wide bridge you see includes later rebuilding and widening. Do not picture the whole roadway as untouched 16th-century stonework.',practical:'Continue north along the river towards Puente del Rey. This is the shorter river option.',kind:'walk'},
 river:{name:'Madrid Río · north',area:'Puente del Rey',lat:40.4202,lng:-3.7226,address:'Puente del Rey',wiki:W('Madrid Río'),story:'Burying sections of the M-30 created room for a public riverside park, with its major transformation completed in 2011. This is the newest layer of our walk.',secret:'Try imagining motorway traffic where you are now walking. City history is also the story of changing priorities.',practical:'Walk Segovia → Rey, then towards Príncipe Pío for lunch and metro. Keep the Casa de Campo lake as a substitute, not another compulsory stop.',kind:'walk'},
 lunch:{name:'Lunch near Príncipe Pío',area:'Príncipe Pío',lat:40.4211,lng:-3.7195,address:'Príncipe Pío · choose a nearby bar',wiki:W('Príncipe Pío (Madrid Metro)'),wikiLabel:'Wikipedia · station',story:'A deliberate gap for food, feet and conversation. Pick a straightforward bar with a menu that suits the group instead of turning lunch into another sightseeing mission.',practical:'Budget €12–20, estimated. For the MAN: L10 to Alonso Martínez, then 15–20 min on foot. That trip stays south of the separate L10 Cuzco closure.',kind:'food'},
 man:{name:'Archaeological Museum',area:'Serrano',lat:40.4233,lng:-3.6888,address:'Calle de Serrano 13',wiki:W('National Archaeological Museum (Madrid)'),story:'A two-hour journey through the peninsula before modern Spain: Iberian sculpture, Roman life and the Visigothic world. Follow your curiosity rather than trying to finish the museum.',secret:'Three things to look for: the Lady of Elche, whose identity is still debated; the human-headed bull of Balazote; and the Guarrazar crowns, religious offerings rather than crowns for wearing.',practical:'Saturday 09:30–20:00; free from 14:00. Our target is 16:00–18:00. Obtain a free ticket if available; capacity still applies.',source:SOURCES.man,tickets:'https://man.sacatuentrada.es/',kind:'museum'},
